{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Take.</b> The opposite of תנו ('give'), except that since a mem follows the form לקיחה ('taking'), here it is like נתינה ('giving'): קחו נא לי מעט מים (1 Kings 17:10), meaning that the taking for me is on my account. Likewise קחו מאתכם תרומה ל HaShem." "And the phrase כל נדיב לבו is construct, like כל אשר נדבו לבו ('all whose heart moves them'), like בגדול זרועך (above 15:16) = בזרועך הגדול, זכר רב טובך (Psalms 145:7) = זכר טובך הרב. And Rabbi Yeshua said that the vav is superfluous, like חיתו שדי (Psalms 104:11), בנו בעור (Numbers 24:3)." "And after mentioning <b>let him bring it.</b> Meaning, he added to clarify that it is a terumah for YHWH, and there are many like it: והוא צוה לי אחי (1 Samuel 20:29)." "Some say silver is so called because it is desirable to the eyes, from the root נכספה (Psalms 84:3); also נחשת (copper), because women divine [מנחשות] with copper."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {0 "The argument hinges on the prefixed mem in מאתכם ('from you'), which Ibn Ezra reads as altering the sense of לקיחה ('taking') toward נתינה ('giving'); translated his phrasing literally without expanding the compressed grammatical point.", 1 "Ibn Ezra's label זרה is obscure; rendered hyper-literally. He may intend it as a grammatical term for the construct adjectival pattern, but this is not certain.", 3 "The folk-etymological pun נחשת / נחש (copper / divination) is rendered literally; Ibn Ezra attributes this to unnamed 'some say' without endorsing it."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:40:38.511006625Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}